Subject: [PATCH] native: Hash.new(map) must return self after populating from a Map

The `native` stdlib reopens `Hash#initialize` so that `Hash.new(source)`
*populates* a fresh Hash from `source`'s entries (instead of using `source` as
the hash's default value). There are two populate branches:

  * `source.constructor === Object|undefined` -> copy keys, then `return self`
  * `source instanceof Map`                   -> copy keys, but NO `return self`

Since Opal 1.8 backs every Ruby Hash with a JS `Map`, passing one Ruby Hash to
`Hash.new` takes the second branch. It populates correctly but then falls
through to the core MRI `Hash#initialize`, which sets the hash's *default value*
to `source`. The result is a populated hash whose every *missing* key returns
the whole `source` hash (a truthy object) instead of `nil`:

    require 'native'
    src  = { 'a' => 1 }
    copy = Hash.new(src)
    copy['a']        # => 1     (populated, good)
    copy['missing']  # => {'a'=>1}  (BUG: should be nil)
    copy.default     # => {'a'=>1}  (BUG: should be nil)

The `Object` branch already guards against this with `return self`; the `Map`
branch is simply missing the same line.

--- a/stdlib/native.rb
+++ b/stdlib/native.rb
@@ -579,6 +579,8 @@ class Hash
           } else if (defaults instanceof Map) {
             Opal.hash_each(defaults, false, function(key, value) {
               $hash_convert_and_put_value(self, key, value);
               return [false, false];
             });
+
+            return self;
           }
         }

         return #{_initialize(defaults, &block)};
